# Schedules, calendars and timers

Use a Schedule helper for weekly on and off blocks, a calendar for dated events and a timer for a countdown. These are different models. Do not show one as another, and do not present a saved schedule, a calendar entry or a running timer as proof that a device did anything.

This is reference specification HA-CTRL-010. The examples are simulated. They do not connect to Home Assistant or control a home. For how to record real testing, see [Native task accessibility and evidence](native-task-accessibility.html).

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-010-capabilities">What each model does</h2>

| Model | What to show |
| --- | --- |
| Schedule | Each block starts at its start time and ends just before its end time. A block from 09:00 to 12:00 is active up to, but not including, 12:00. Blocks on the same day cannot overlap. |
| Overnight block | A block cannot cross midnight. Show two touching blocks on consecutive days, for example 22:00 to 24:00 and then 00:00 to 05:00. |
| Touching blocks | The schedule stays on across the boundary. Only the block data can change, so do not show an off and on event. |
| Calendar | Keep all-day dates apart from timed events. Show only the creating, editing and repeating that the calendar integration supports. A calendar entity is On only while an event is active. |
| Timer | Show the duration and one of three states: idle, active or paused. A timer returns to idle when it finishes, is cancelled or has not started. |
| Time input | Name the time zone next to the time. Keep the person's own clock and locale settings. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-010-states">Saved, pending and failed</h2>

Show unsaved edits apart from the schedule that is currently saved. Reject an overlap or an end before a start at the field that caused it, and keep what the person typed. Cancel discards the draft only. It does not stop anything that is already running. Say before saving that a changed helper can change the conditions an automation uses.

Show an unavailable calendar as unavailable. Do not show it as an empty day.

A timer can be restored after Home Assistant restarts, but only if it was set up with the restore option. Home Assistant restores active and paused timers. If a timer ends while Home Assistant is stopped, an automation that waits for the `timer.finished` event does not run at startup. Tell the person when that can happen. Keep the paused time apart from any estimated finish time.

For clock changes, test a local time that does not exist and one that happens twice. The Schedule documentation does not give one rule for every case, so record what the installed version does and do not invent a rule.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-010-content">Wording</h2>

Say what the end time means. For example: “Ends at 12:00. 12:00 is not part of this block.” Name the time zone beside the editing controls. A viewer's browser zone and the zone used by the installation can differ, so show both when they do.

Show a repeating rule as a readable sentence and offer only the exceptions the integration can save. Label an event date, a publication date and a timer state separately. An all-day event is a date, not midnight converted to the viewer's zone.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-010-accessibility">Keyboard and screen reader use</h2>

Let people create and edit blocks without dragging. Give each time field a label that includes the day and whether it is the start or the end, and tie its error message to it. Offer a list view of calendar events alongside any grid, with dates and all-day events intact. Do not announce every second of a countdown. Test a repeated hour, narrow screens and larger text.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-010-integration">Keep the native editors</h2>

Keep the native Schedule, Calendar and Timer editors, their saved settings and their event rules. These examples show review states only and are not a scheduler. Do not add a script or helper just to make an example interactive.

